The flutter_app test is stuck in plugin resolution warnings loop (flutter_inappwebview) — this is the `flutter test` startup (pub/plugin resolution). The earlier run (pre-fix) DID complete in ~2-4 min... Actually did it? The earlier runs each took a couple of minutes and completed. This run started 14:24; sh-175 still running with timeout 180 — it will terminate at 180s via `timeout`.

The plugin warnings look like normal startup noise (each `flutter test` run prints them). The earlier successful runs took ~2-3 min. The stuck run earlier (sh-158) was the anomaly — probably my flicker test hanging: after `releaseSecond.complete()`, `await sending` — hmm, or maybe the widget test's runAsync + my streaming gate: the poll loop exits when the assistant row exists (imediately); but the `await tester.pump()` after runAsync — fine.

Wait, actually there's a real deadlock candidate in my test: `unawaited(releaseSecond.future.then(...))` — the completion of releaseSecond happens inside `tester.runAsync` — then pushes happen — but `tester.runAsync` requires ALL fake-async timers... The Agent runs on real async (runAsync). The `.then` callback runs on the event loop — OK.

But: `expect(...)` throwing INSIDE the runAsync closure aborts the closure → `releaseSecond.complete()` never runs → `await sending` (skipped due to throw) — the test framework waits for... the zone? runAsync waits for the returned future which threw → test fails fast. That's the pre-fix behavior (it failed in ~2min). So sh-158's 10-min empty log was odd — maybe it HUNG on `sending` never completing because the expect FAILED silently?... The exception propagates; release never completes; the flutter test runner waits for the pending `sendText` future (the agent still awaits stream events — runAsync keeps the test alive while real async work is pending!). THAT's the hang: after the expect throws, `releaseSecond` never completes, the agent's stream never ends, and the test runner waits indefinitely for the outstanding runAsync work. Classic.

Fix the test: complete the release in a `try/finally` so a failing expect still releases, and add a timeout on `sending` await. Let me restructure: move the flicker expect AFTER release (capture the streamed content first into a variable), i.e.:

```dart
final streamedContent = row?.content;
releaseSecond.complete();
await sending.timeout(30s);
// then asserts
``

That way the release ALWAYS happens before assertions. Let me rewrite the test body accordingly. Also — `timeout 180` will kill the current stuck run; wait for it, fix the test, rerun.

